< Forcing 768 tokens raises $q$ by 0.11--0.18 (all intervals exclude zero after a Bonferroni correction), almost all of it above 256 tokens, and recovers 42--85\% of the calculator's lead (Table~\ref{tab:main}); budget-zero answers ($q=0.72$--0.78) are within about 0.06 of random. Re-scoring the same choices under a lognormal fit to held-out prices gives 44--85\%, and under a win curve consistent with eBay's mechanism, where a new bidder must beat the highest of each auction's $n$ rival values (a lognormal value distribution fitted by maximum likelihood to closing prices as second-highest values), 43--87\%; with rivals bidding the symmetric first-price equilibrium for that value distribution, 40--85\%. The unforced run mostly measures more tokens: 46--87\% of its traces reach the 1{,}024 cap for five models, while Granite-8B always stops, by 669 tokens in 90\% of traces, and scores slightly better unforced (0.912) than forced to 768 (0.897). Dollar recovery is lower for five models because Cartier dominates the dollar gap.
